Week 5 — Advanced Security and Monitoring Infrastructure
← Week 4 | Week 5 | Week 6 →
Overview
Week 5 focuses on implementing advanced security controls and developing server monitoring infrastructure. This includes enforcing mandatory access control, automating updates, deploying intrusion detection, and creating scripts to verify security configurations and collect performance metrics remotely.
All configurations and monitoring are performed via SSH from the workstation to the headless server.
Objectives
Implement mandatory access control using AppArmor or SELinux
Enable automated security updates
Configure fail2ban for intrusion detection
Develop security-baseline.sh to verify system security
Develop monitor-server.sh for remote performance monitoring
Document all commands, outputs, and configuration changes
Mandatory Access Control (AppArmor)
Installed and enabled AppArmor:
sudo apt install -y apparmor apparmor-utils
sudo systemctl enable apparmor
sudo systemctl start apparmor
sudo aa-status
Verified enforced profiles for system applications:
sudo aa-enforce /etc/apparmor.d/*
Evidence:
aa-status output showing profiles in enforce mode
Logs of any access denials captured
Learning:
AppArmor enforces least-privilege access per process, reducing the impact of exploited services
Automated Security Updates
Installed unattended-upgrades package:
sudo apt install -y unattended-upgrades
sudo dpkg-reconfigure --priority=low unattended-upgrades
Verified automatic updates are scheduled and enabled:
systemctl status unattended-upgrades
Evidence:
Screenshot showing active unattended-upgrades service
Logs confirming recent security updates applied
Intrusion Detection with fail2ban
Installed fail2ban:
sudo apt install -y fail2ban
Configured jail for SSH:
sudo nano /etc/fail2ban/jail.local
# [sshd]
# enabled = true
# port = ssh
# filter = sshd
# logpath = /var/log/auth.log
# maxretry = 3
Restarted fail2ban service:
sudo systemctl restart fail2ban
sudo fail2ban-client status sshd
Evidence:
Screenshot showing active fail2ban jail and banned IPs
Logs showing blocked failed login attempts
Security Baseline Verification Script (security-baseline.sh)
Purpose:
Verify all security configurations implemented in Weeks 4–5
Check SSH settings, firewall rules, AppArmor status, fail2ban, and user privileges
Example Script Snippet:
#!/bin/bash
# security-baseline.sh
# Verifies server security configuration

echo "Checking SSH configuration..."
grep -E "PermitRootLogin|PasswordAuthentication" /etc/ssh/sshd_config

echo "Checking firewall status..."
sudo ufw status verbose

echo "Checking AppArmor profiles..."
sudo aa-status

echo "Checking fail2ban status..."
sudo fail2ban-client status sshd

echo "Checking admin user privileges..."
sudo -l -U adminuser
Evidence:
Script executed via SSH from workstation
Terminal outputs showing pass/fail for each check
Remote Monitoring Script (monitor-server.sh)
Purpose:
Collect server performance metrics remotely from the workstation
Automate monitoring for CPU, memory, disk I/O, and network usage
Example Script Snippet:
#!/bin/bash
# monitor-server.sh
# Collects remote server metrics

SERVER="adminuser@192.168.56.103"

echo "CPU Usage:"
ssh $SERVER "top -b -n1 | head -10"

echo "Memory Usage:"
ssh $SERVER "free -h"

echo "Disk Usage:"
ssh $SERVER "df -h"

echo "Network Connections:"
ssh $SERVER "ss -tuln"
Evidence:
Screenshots of metrics collected via SSH
Logged outputs stored for later performance analysis
